WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.517
Security risks specific to coding agents.

2
00:00:02.797 --> 00:00:14.659
A coding agent combines three things that are each manageable alone: it reads untrusted text, it has access to your code and often your secrets, and it can run commands and reach the network.

3
00:00:14.939 --> 00:00:25.307
Simon Willison’s lethal trifecta names the dangerous combination: private data, exposure to untrusted content, and the ability to communicate externally.

4
00:00:25.587 --> 00:00:32.029
When all three meet, an attacker who controls some of the content can try to steer the agent into sending the data out.

5
00:00:32.309 --> 00:00:34.891
Prompt injection through what the agent reads.

6
00:00:35.171 --> 00:00:45.731
Greshake and colleagues (2023) showed indirect injection: instructions planted in content an LLM application retrieves can hijack it.

7
00:00:46.011 --> 00:00:59.963
For a coding agent, the content is everywhere: a README or code comment in a dependency, an issue or pull request description, a web page it fetched for documentation, a tool result from an MCP server.

8
00:01:00.243 --> 00:01:14.408
Invariant Labs reported an attack on the GitHub MCP integration in which a malicious issue in a public repository led an agent to pull data from the user’s private repositories into a public pull request.

9
00:01:14.688 --> 00:01:24.885
Codex’s docs make the same point from the vendor side: they default web search to a cached index to reduce exposure, and tell you to treat web results as untrusted.

10
00:01:25.165 --> 00:01:40.355
What Claude Code does about it, per its security docs: network commands like curl and wget aren’t auto-approved by default; most web fetches go through a separate model call that summarizes the page rather than handing Claude the raw text;

11
00:01:40.635 --> 00:01:47.952
a workspace trust dialog appears in folders you haven’t trusted; project MCP servers need their own approval.

12
00:01:48.232 --> 00:02:01.011
In auto mode, the classifier blocks by default things like downloading and executing code, sending sensitive data to external endpoints, production deploys, force pushes, and destructive git commands,

13
00:02:01.291 --> 00:02:09.035
and the best-practices page lists actions that look driven by content Claude read rather than by you among the things it watches for.

14
00:02:09.315 --> 00:02:18.680
The docs are also clear that no system is immune and that auto mode is a model’s judgment, so your “never” rules belong in deny rules and hooks.

15
00:02:18.960 --> 00:02:19.856
Secrets.

16
00:02:20.136 --> 00:02:21.757
Deny rules such as Read(.

17
00:02:22.037 --> 00:02:22.869
/.

18
00:02:23.149 --> 00:02:24.259
env) and Read(.

19
00:02:24.539 --> 00:02:38.299
/secrets/**) block Claude’s file tools and recognized shell file commands, but not a script that opens the file itself; the sandbox adds OS-level enforcement for the commands it wraps.

20
00:02:38.579 --> 00:02:46.728
Better still, keep production secrets off the machine the agent runs on, and give it scoped, revocable credentials for test environments.

21
00:02:47.008 --> 00:03:01.152
Willison’s advice for running agents with few prompts is to use exactly that: credentials for test or staging, tight budgets on anything that can spend money, and a container or remote environment rather than your laptop.

22
00:03:01.432 --> 00:03:02.733
Destructive commands.

23
00:03:03.013 --> 00:03:05.808
Checkpoints don’t undo remote effects (lesson 1).

24
00:03:06.088 --> 00:03:18.824
Keep force pushes, migrations, deploys, and deletions behind a person: deny or ask rules for the commands, branch protection on main, and, for unattended runs, a disposable environment.

25
00:03:19.104 --> 00:03:29.323
And remember that repository configuration is itself an attack surface: a cloned repo’s hooks run in claude -p sessions without a trust dialog, so read.

26
00:03:29.603 --> 00:03:30.755
claude/ and.

27
00:03:31.035 --> 00:03:32.037
mcp.

28
00:03:32.317 --> 00:03:35.731
json before running an agent on code you didn’t write.
